      // [src_durer_conway, src_durer_met, src_durer_self_conway | 1511; 1513-14; July 1520 | scholar + museum + self] Conway describes him setting up his own press and in 1511 publishing four woodcut books at once with the imprint 'Printed at Nürnberg by Albrecht Dürer, the painter', and selling prints from a stall at the Frankfurt and Nuremberg fairs. Wisse (Met) notes that the 1513-14 master engravings were aimed 'more for connoisseurs and collectors than for popular devotion'. On the 1520 journey his diary records giving a painting, the Life of the Virgin, the Apocalypse and engravings to the Bishop of Bamberg, who gave him a toll pass and letters of introduction, and repeatedly trading prints for goods and services. Conway's reading of the altarpieces as advertising is not used.
